st  it's insane it's insane and it's  heartbreaking  and and i just it was a very cathartic  um moment for me um and release  for me and i just wanted to give a voice  to it  and you talk about talking with sons and  explaining  how they're going to be perceived in the  world growing up  those are tough conversations do you  feel it's a different  conversation now with this generation  i don't think the conversation is uh is  different  i think that  the difference is that it is this  generation  who are having these conversations with  their parents  and they're not understanding it and um  i think they are rising up to speak  against it because  as we're all home uh during this  pandemic  yeah uh everyone  could not um turn away from  what they saw on television uh when that  happened to george floyd  and you know i say if you are i don't  care  what your skin tone is if you are a  human  being and you have a heart that affected  you  um and i don't think it just  happened and affected just black people  there are many many other ethnicities  that it affected and they are  all speaking out about it now i think  that's the difference  you know when you think about that um  social media  media and it there's a line in your song  where you say i don't want to watch  tv and see another see another casualty  and it reminds me of will smith and what  he said you know racism hasn't  uh what's the actual quote he says  racism is not getting worse  it's just getting filmed and when you  think of that these are all trigger  points  when you're on social media and watching  the news  how do you take care of yourself  mentally to make sure  you're okay i  um i i meditate and i pray a lot and i  also write in my gratitude journal um  that's what it takes for me um to rise  above  uh the reality that i'm  in and that the way things appear to be  and it gives me the strength to  be able to see it from another  perspective in order to try and deal  with it  um that's  that's how 